  "Question": "If \\(x^{2m+1}\\div x^m=x^5\\) (\\(x\\ne0\\)), what is the value of \\(m\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The condition \\(x\\ne0\\) still allows \\(x=1\\). For this base,\n\\[\n1^{2m+1}\\div1^m=1=1^5,\n\\]\nso both \\(m=1\\) and \\(m=4\\), for example, satisfy the displayed equation. Thus the stated conditions do not determine a unique value of \\(m\\).\n\nIf the equality is instead intended as a formal identity in \\(x\\), the left side equals \\(x^{2m+1-m}=x^{m+1}\\). Comparing exponents in that identity gives \\(m+1=5\\), so \\(m=4\\). Under this additional interpretation the answer is \\(4\\); as written, \\(m\\) is not uniquely determined.",
